# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Orbit — email delivery (SMTP) credentials
#
# Copy the vars you need into your main .env to switch Orbit's
# password-reset and team-invite links from "shown on-screen only" to
# actually emailed (the on-screen link still shows as a fallback if SMTP
# is unset or a send fails).
#
# Behaviour:
#   • ALL five SMTP_HOST / SMTP_PORT / SMTP_USER / SMTP_PASS / EMAIL_FROM
#     set → real SMTP send via nodemailer.
#   • Any of them missing → sendEmail() logs + no-ops (dev/demo safe); the
#     app keeps showing the reset/invite link on-screen exactly as before.
#
# See lib/email.ts (EMAIL_SETUP_DOC) for the full walkthrough.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# SMTP server hostname, e.g. smtp.sendgrid.net, smtp-relay.gmail.com,
# smtp.mailgun.org, email-smtp.us-east-1.amazonaws.com (SES).
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.sendgrid.net

# SMTP port — 587 (STARTTLS, recommended) or 465 (implicit TLS).
# SMTP_PORT=587

# SMTP auth username (often "apikey" for SendGrid, or your SES/Mailgun user).
# SMTP_USER=apikey

# SMTP auth password / API key — server-only, NEVER expose to the browser.
# SMTP_PASS=your-smtp-password-or-api-key

# "From" address shown on outgoing mail. Must be a domain/address verified
# with your provider, e.g. "Orbit <no-reply@yourdomain.com>".
# EMAIL_FROM="Orbit <no-reply@yourdomain.com>"

# ── Where to get SMTP credentials ──
# 1. Sign up with a transactional email provider: SendGrid, Postmark, Mailgun,
#    Amazon SES, Resend (SMTP mode) — or use Gmail with an App Password for
#    quick local testing.
# 2. Verify a sending domain (or single sender) with that provider.
# 3. Copy the SMTP host/port/username/password they issue into the vars above.
#
# lib/notify.ts's generic EMAIL_WEBHOOK_URL webhook path still works as an
# alternative if you'd rather POST to your own email microservice instead of
# using SMTP directly — SMTP via lib/email.ts is preferred when both are set.
